    <!--
      Bottom-bar / header hairline (outlineVariant read too prominent). Picked
      by EFFECTIVE darkness in code (not a -night qualifier, because incognito
      is always dark regardless of system theme): a soft translucent-BLACK line
      on a light surface (faint grey), a soft translucent-WHITE line on any dark
      surface (system-dark near-black OR incognito purple) - a black groove is
      invisible on a dark surface, so dark always uses white.
    -->
    <color name="bottom_bar_divider_light">#14000000</color>
    <color name="bottom_bar_divider_dark">#14FFFFFF</color>


    <!-- Checked filter chip fill + label. Aliases here rather than the raw
         palette attrs so each theme can hold its own value: light theme wants
         the light container tone, dark theme does NOT (see values-night).

         This is the CHIP's tone and nothing else's. It was briefly shared with
         the buy-credit segments under a role name, which was a mistake worth
         not repeating: a filter chip sits on a list rail with no primary action
         to be subordinate to, so it can afford the full brand; a segmented
         control sitting directly above a CTA cannot, and wearing this tone put
         it ΔE 8.2 from the Continue button — close enough to read as the same
         colour. Same visual ROLE, opposite CONSTRAINT. See
         @color/buy_segment_checked_container. -->
    <color name="chip_checked_container">#EC7E78</color>
    <color name="chip_checked_on_container">#460005</color>

    <!-- Checked segment of the buy-credit duration / pay-rail toggles: a TONAL
         fill, not a brand one. The whole buy screen is a hierarchy problem —
         two segmented toggles and a filled Continue CTA stacked in one column —
         and Material's own segmented button is tonal for exactly this reason.
         "Selected" reads from FILL-VERSUS-OUTLINE against its unchecked
         neighbour, which never needed saturation to carry it, so the brand fill
         can stay unique to the one primary action.

         #FBD5D3 is ΔE 16.8 off the page (plainly a fill) and ΔE 48.3 off the
         #f0716c CTA (unmistakably not the button). The label #682423 clears
         8.34:1 on it, and the "−25%" badge inherits that same colour, so the
         badge is legible checked and unchecked without a colour span.
         Night value in values-night — and it INVERTS there, deliberately. -->
    <color name="buy_segment_checked_container">#FBD5D3</color>
    <color name="buy_segment_checked_on_container">#682423</color>
    <!-- Progress-bar indicator. Deliberately NOT colorPrimary in light theme.
         A determinate bar has to separate from its own track, and against
         #f0716c that is mathematically impossible with a lighter track: pure
         white only reaches 2.88:1, under the 3:1 WCAG 1.4.11 bar for a graphic
         boundary. So the indicator darkens instead — #C24941 clears 3.82:1
         against the #f0716c@20% track these bars use. Dark theme keeps the
         brand value, which already clears it at 4.80:1. -->
    <color name="progress_indicator">#C24941</color>
